apps/operator/public/internal/docs.old.*/

# EI-19341904812190592: starlight-llms-txt's full/small dumps of the whole
# engineering docs corpus (~11MB + ~8MB, regenerated wholesale by every
# `docs:rebuild`). Committing them re-adds ~19MB of new incompressible blob
# per docs rebuild and was measured throttling the p2p own-head publish leg
# fleet-wide (500MB/tick byte cap → ~26 commits/tick while inside this region
# of history). They are build output — postbuild-copy.sh regenerates them
# from apps/operator-docs/dist on every docs:rebuild — so untrack instead of
# deleting: `git rm --cached` them once, then this pattern keeps them out of
# git-sync's `git add -A` going forward. llms.txt itself is a tiny (~600B)
# index page and stays tracked.
apps/operator/public/internal/docs/llms-full.txt
apps/operator/public/internal/docs/llms-small.txt

# WI-3336: esbuild-bundled host entry (bundle-host.sh) — built per bg-host restart
apps/operator/dist-host/
# Sibling of dist-host: the esbuild-bundled DESKTOP SIDECAR (embed-sidecar.mjs, ~51MB) + the
# blueprint copies the embed build stages beside it. Generated output, never source — it was
# swept into git by accident (2026-07-11) and every tree-scanning guard (no-raw-harness-sentinel,
# drop-database-force, envelope-grammar, undrained-stdout) then reddened the gate on bundle text
# (EI-22059281687580461, WI-2139971, WI-2139976). Untracked 2026-09-02 (WI-2141120).
apps/operator/dist-sidecar/

# WI-4481: flake-soak "prove it discriminates" fixtures. A flake fix drops a
# KNOWN-FLAKY *.flakeproof.test.tsx next to the code under test and runs
# `scripts/flake-soak.sh --self-test <fixture>` to prove the soak lane can
# actually redden a load-sensitive race. They are deliberately red, so they must
# never reach the tree (CI would go red) — the package vitest.config also
# excludes them from every run except the soak's own self-test.
**/*.flakeproof.test.ts
**/*.flakeproof.test.tsx

# Vitest junit reporter output (2026-08-24, WI-41047 triage): stray in-tree
# junit.xml files get swept in by git-sync's blanket `git add -A`, and their
# embedded test names red-pinned the envelope-grammar-cross-language recurrence
# guard (apps/operator/junit.xml, 556KB, committed ad43f56d27). Six were tracked
# (root, apps/operator, apps/operator-vite, packages/operator-core, libs/flags,
# packages/plugin-loader) — all removed; this keeps the class out everywhere.
junit.xml
**/junit.xml

# Whisper voice-service scratch recordings. The local whisper service writes an
# 8-byte probe .wav into its runtime CWD (packages/operator-core) on each
# invocation and never cleans them up; git-sync's blanket `git add -A` was
# committing the junk and re-dirtying the tree continuously, which blocks every
# release cut (release-local.sh refuses a -dirty superproject). Ignore them.
whisper-server-*.wav

# WI-4776: a browser-extension probe (claude-in-chrome setup tooling, external to
# this repo) creates a temp `probehome.<random>/` HOME-probe directory at the repo
# root containing REAL machine paths (NativeMessagingHosts manifests with this
# box's actual $HOME baked in). git-sync's blanket `git add -A` committed one of
# these on 2026-07-13 and it sat tracked in the tree leaking a real home path
# until removed — the same identity-leak CLASS check-no-box-identity.mjs /
# check-no-identity-literals.mjs exist to prevent, just via an untracked-junk path
# instead of authored source. Ignore the whole class so it can never recur.
probehome.*/

# Playwright MCP session snapshots — agent browser-session debris, never source.
# Carried owner-identity UI text into the 0.0.12 cut (WI-4776 lint); keep untracked.
.playwright-mcp/

# AppImage extractions (`--appimage-extract`) unpack the ENTIRE bundled app: a
# 90MB libwebkit2gtk .so, 64MB corestore .sst files, zellij/kopia binaries, a
# 75MB git bundle. A perf test left one at tools/perf-test/wdio/.appimage-extract
# on 2026-07-20 and git-sync's blanket `git add -A` committed 2.26 GB of it
# (46c2a163d6), pushing the pot-git publish guard's new-bytes total to 2301 MB —
# past its 500 MB cap. The guard then refused EVERY publish, and since its
# baseline only advances THROUGH a successful publish, the refusal was
# self-perpetuating: deleting the files 10 minutes later did nothing (the blobs
# stay reachable from a commit inside the judged range) and origin/staging sat
# frozen for 5 days. Never source — ignore the whole class so it cannot recur.
**/.appimage-extract/
**/squashfs-root/

# Composed rig-VM sidecar — build OUTPUT, never source. `bin/vm-rig/
# compose-rig-sidecar-current.sh` defaults its OUT to $REPO_ROOT/.rig-sidecar-current
# (dot-prefixed, so it is easy to miss in a status), and every run re-emits a
# 58MB esbuild bundle plus verbatim COPIES of all ~549 db-sql migrations and the
# prompts tree. git-sync's blanket `git add -A` then committed 762 files of it on
# 2026-07-26, and logged the oversize warning
#   "EXCLUDED oversized file(s) … they will never push: .rig-sidecar-current/serve.mjs (58.1MB)"
# on every single tick. Same class as the .appimage-extract entry above — a
# regenerable build artifact landing in the tree because nothing ignored it.
# Regenerate it any time with the compose script; it must never be tracked.
.rig-sidecar-current/

# GitNexus code-graph index — a regenerable ARTIFACT, never source. Same class as
# the two entries above, and armed the same way: `npx gitnexus analyze` (the setup
# step libs/papercusp/plugins/gitnexus-bridge's README tells you to run once per
# repo) writes its whole graph DB to <repo>/.gitnexus/, and nothing ignored it. A
# 121-file repo's index is already 27MB; this tree has ~11k tracked TS/TSX files,
# so the first analyze here would hand git-sync's blanket `git add -A` an index
# orders of magnitude past the pot-git publish guard's 500MB cap — the exact
# self-perpetuating freeze documented in the .appimage-extract entry above.
# Ignored BEFORE the first index rather than after (WI-6445).
.gitnexus/

# V8 heap snapshots — a DIAGNOSTIC artifact, never source. Same class as the
# entries above, but arriving from a different direction: they are written by
# hand during a memory investigation (`--heapsnapshot-signal`, the inspector's
# "take heap snapshot", or a heapdump call), land wherever the process's cwd
# happened to be — usually the repo ROOT — and are named for the timestamp, so
# each one is a NEW path that no previous ignore covers. A single snapshot of a
# process this size is ~131MB, past GitHub's 100MB limit, so git-sync excludes it
# and broadcasts "EXCLUDED oversized file(s) … they will never push" to the whole
# fleet on EVERY tick for as long as it sits there (observed 2026-07-27 for
# Heap.20260727.161321.85110.0.001.heapsnapshot, 130.6MB).
# Ignoring the PATTERN rather than the file matters here: the next investigation
# writes a differently-named snapshot and would re-arm the same fleet-wide noise.
# Note this only silences git — the snapshot still occupies disk, and is still
# its author's to delete when the investigation is done (WI-6463).
*.heapsnapshot

# Console liveness markers — per-PID, per-MACHINE runtime state, never source.
# `console-spawn.ts:372` opens every console with `touch .papercup-console-active.$$`
# and a `trap 'rm -f …' EXIT`, so the marker names the live console's pid and is how
# a leftover window's pid is recovered after its agent exits. The trap only runs on a
# CLEAN exit: a console killed by SIGKILL (or lost with the host) leaks its marker
# forever, and each leak is a NEW path named for a pid that no previous ignore covers
# — the same pattern-not-path argument as the heapsnapshot entry above.
# Nothing ignored them, so git-sync's blanket `git add -A` has been committing them:
